================================================================================
   LAYERED LAW OF STOP
================================================================================
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | L1 - DEVELOPER STOP                                        |
   |   Authority: PROVIDER RESPONSIBLE OFFICER (distinct from   |
   |              the Agency Responsible Officer)               |
   |   Trigger:   internal red-team finding of misalignment or  |
   |              catastrophic risk                             |
   |   Technical: documented hard-lock within 15 minutes        |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | L2 - REGULATOR STOP                                        |
   |   Authority: Independent Registry Authority                |
   |   Trigger:   breach of pre-registered failure thresholds   |
   |   Technical: enforceable access to model weights and       |
   |              inference logs                                |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | L3 - INFRASTRUCTURE STOP                                   |
   |   Authority: designated critical-infrastructure authority  |
   |   Trigger:   L1 and L2 failure, or systemic cascade risk   |
   |   Technical: physical air-gap capability at data-centre    |
   |              level                                         |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | L4 - JUDICIAL STOP                                         |
   |   Authority: court order, including ex-parte urgency       |
   |   Trigger:   any of the above, plus urgency                |
   |   Technical: independent expert lot-drawn audit within     |
   |              72 hours                                      |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | SAFE STATE / DEGRADED MODE                                 |
   |   Every stop must transition the system to a defined SAFE  |
   |   STATE, not merely halt it. Safe state means:             |
   |     - no further consequential actions                     |
   |     - state preserved for forensic audit                   |
   |     - no data destruction or model-weight alteration       |
   |   A DEGRADED MODE is available where a full stop would     |
   |   cause greater harm (for example life-support routing).   |
   |   Degraded mode permits only protective, containment or    |
   |   non-expansive acts.                                      |
   |   The choice between safe state and degraded mode must be  |
   |   justified in writing and published to the Registry       |
   |   within 6 hours.                                          |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | RESTART AUTHORITY                                          |
   |   Restart after a stop requires:                           |
   |     (a) a written root-cause report, AND                   |
   |     (b) approval by the authority that ordered the stop,   |
   |         or by the IRA if the ordering authority is          |
   |         unavailable, AND                                   |
   |     (c) a 24-hour cooling-off period after the root-cause  |
   |         report is published.                               |
   |   Restart without these steps = unauthorized processing.   |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | L1-L4 DISAGREEMENT RULE                                    |
   |   If two or more layers disagree on whether to stop, the   |
   |   MORE RESTRICTIVE position prevails, subject to the       |
   |   safe-state rule, until the disagreement is resolved by   |
   |   the IRA within 24 hours.                                 |
   |   No layer may override another layer unilaterally.        |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | TIERED REACHABILITY TARGETS                                |
   |   safety-critical : 15 minutes                             |
   |   other high-risk : 2 hours                                |
   |   general-purpose : 24 hours                               |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | DRILLS - SHADOW / STAGING ONLY                             |
   |   Unannounced drills on LIVE safety-critical systems are   |
   |   PROHIBITED. Drills run on SHADOW or STAGING environments |
   |   that mirror production.                                  |
   |   Semi-annual unannounced drills in shadow mode.           |
   |   Results INDEPENDENTLY VERIFIED.                          |
   |   Drills must include STOP-FAILURE SCENARIOS: a simulated  |
   |   L1-L4 failure with a demonstrated containment fallback.  |
   |   PROVIDER drill failure -> immediate revocation of        |
   |   provider certification AND an automatic Article 12       |
   |   full-cycle audit for the DEPLOYING AGENCY. Revocation    |
   |   takes effect after a transition period where systems     |
   |   are live.                                                |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
                                   |
                                   v
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | MANDATORY EX-POST AUDIT (within 15 days)                   |
   |   lot-drawn accredited experts; public report to           |
   |   Parliament and the Data Protection Authority             |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | PUBLIC REVIEW                                              |
   |   parliamentary debate OR mini-public review of:           |
   |     - whether the emergency was justified                  |
   |     - whether pre-registered indicators performed          |
   |     - lessons fed back into future metric design           |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | PROHIBITION ON PRECEDENT                                   |
   |   Emergency outputs may NOT be used as precedent for       |
   |   future normal-path decisions without a fresh Article 12  |
   |   framing audit.                                           |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | VENDOR DUTIES                                              |
   |   Providers must:                                          |
   |     (a) tag all emergency-path outputs with a              |
   |         machine-readable provenance marker                 |
   |     (b) maintain a tamper-evident training-data manifest   |
   |         (hashed lists, retained internally or under        |
   |         escrow) so that claims can be tested without       |
   |         public release of full datasets                    |
   |     (c) honour provenance tags in downstream training      |
   |         pipelines                                          |
   +------------------------------------------------------------+
   | EMERGENCY TRAINING BAR                                     |
   |   No model may be trained on emergency-path output unless  |
   |   a fresh Article 12 framing audit has been completed for  |
   |   that specific use.                                       |
   |   A model trained on emergency output without such an      |
   |   audit is unauthorized processing.                        |
   |   A model that discovers downstream that it was trained    |
   |   on untagged emergency output must (i) suspend             |
   |   consequential use, (ii) re-register with corrected       |
   |   provenance, and (iii) seek a fresh Article 12 audit.     |
